1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,831
Half a turn of a bicycle wheel is
one hundred and eighty degrees.

2
00:00:04,831 --> 00:00:07,737
Last time we found it is also pi radians.

3
00:00:08,137 --> 00:00:10,233
Same turn, two different numbers.

4
00:00:10,683 --> 00:00:14,624
Now Aarav's cycling app says
his wheel turned three radians.

5
00:00:14,974 --> 00:00:17,461
Is that more than half a turn, or less?

6
00:00:17,461 --> 00:00:20,299
He cannot tell, because
the units do not match.

7
00:00:20,799 --> 00:00:25,206
To switch between degrees and radians,
you need one fact and two simple

8
00:00:25,206 --> 00:00:26,106
rules.

9
00:00:26,456 --> 00:00:28,127
Start with one full turn.

10
00:00:28,477 --> 00:00:31,854
In degrees, a full turn is
three hundred and sixty.

11
00:00:31,854 --> 00:00:33,746
In radians, it is two pi.

12
00:00:34,146 --> 00:00:38,211
So two pi radians equals three
hundred and sixty degrees.

13
00:00:38,611 --> 00:00:39,942
Halve both sides.

14
00:00:39,942 --> 00:00:43,251
Pi radians equals one
hundred and eighty degrees.

15
00:00:43,251 --> 00:00:45,612
This one fact does all the work.

16
00:00:46,262 --> 00:00:49,141
Watch one turn measured both ways at once.

17
00:00:49,141 --> 00:00:51,227
The outer ring counts degrees.

18
00:00:51,227 --> 00:00:53,312
The inner ring counts radians.

19
00:00:53,712 --> 00:00:56,906
At a quarter turn, ninety
degrees, and pi by two.

20
00:00:57,306 --> 00:01:00,584
At half a turn, one hundred
and eighty degrees, and pi.

21
00:01:00,984 --> 00:01:05,141
All the way round, three hundred
and sixty degrees, and two pi.

22
00:01:05,141 --> 00:01:06,970
Every angle has both names.

23
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:11,189
Now let's find the size of
one radian, and of one degree.

24
00:01:11,539 --> 00:01:12,450
Step one.

25
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:15,982
Pi radians equals one
hundred and eighty degrees.

26
00:01:16,732 --> 00:01:17,661
Step two.

27
00:01:17,661 --> 00:01:19,644
Divide both sides by pi.

28
00:01:19,644 --> 00:01:24,720
One radian is one eighty over pi
degrees, about fifty seven point three

29
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:25,620
degrees.

30
00:01:26,274 --> 00:01:27,235
Step three.

31
00:01:27,235 --> 00:01:31,330
Go back to step one, and divide
both sides by one eighty.

32
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:34,235
One degree is pi over one eighty radians.

33
00:01:34,985 --> 00:01:38,787
That is about zero point
zero one seven five radians.

34
00:01:38,787 --> 00:01:40,807
A degree is a tiny angle.

35
00:01:40,807 --> 00:01:42,665
A radian is a big one.

36
00:01:43,415 --> 00:01:44,858
This gives two rules.

37
00:01:45,258 --> 00:01:49,008
Degrees to radians: multiply
by pi over one eighty.

38
00:01:49,758 --> 00:01:53,651
Radians to degrees: multiply
by one eighty over pi.

39
00:01:54,401 --> 00:01:59,358
To remember: going into radians, you
want pi to appear, so pi goes on top.

40
00:02:00,008 --> 00:02:03,221
Use the rule on the angles
you will meet most often.

41
00:02:03,621 --> 00:02:05,883
Thirty degrees is pi by six.

42
00:02:05,883 --> 00:02:07,975
Forty five is pi by four.

43
00:02:07,975 --> 00:02:09,775
Sixty is pi by three.

44
00:02:09,775 --> 00:02:11,519
Ninety is pi by two.

45
00:02:12,119 --> 00:02:13,574
One eighty is pi.

46
00:02:13,574 --> 00:02:15,984
Two seventy is three pi by two.

47
00:02:15,984 --> 00:02:18,058
And three sixty is two pi.

48
00:02:18,708 --> 00:02:19,608
Quick one.

49
00:02:19,515 --> 00:02:22,375
Which is bigger: one
radian, or one degree?

50
00:02:22,375 --> 00:02:23,516
Pause and think.

51
00:02:26,716 --> 00:02:28,362
One radian, by far!

52
00:02:28,362 --> 00:02:30,724
It is about fifty seven degrees.

53
00:02:31,374 --> 00:02:32,524
Remember this.

54
00:02:32,524 --> 00:02:35,943
Pi radians equals one
hundred and eighty degrees.

55
00:02:36,643 --> 00:02:37,736
Side by side.

56
00:02:37,736 --> 00:02:42,077
Pi on its own is a number, about
three point one four one six.

57
00:02:42,477 --> 00:02:47,646
Pi radians is an angle: half a turn,
or one hundred and eighty degrees.

58
00:02:47,646 --> 00:02:50,295
So pi is never equal to one eighty.

59
00:02:50,295 --> 00:02:51,833
Only pi radians is.

60
00:02:52,433 --> 00:02:53,429
Now you try.

61
00:02:53,429 --> 00:02:55,605
Aarav's wheel turned three radians.

62
00:02:55,605 --> 00:02:58,351
Is that more than half a turn, or less?

63
00:02:58,351 --> 00:02:59,541
Pause and think.

64
00:03:02,741 --> 00:03:03,641
Less!

65
00:03:03,290 --> 00:03:06,479
Half a turn is pi, about
three point one four.

66
00:03:06,479 --> 00:03:11,423
Three radians is just short of it, about
one hundred and seventy two degrees.

67
00:03:12,073 --> 00:03:13,809
Now, one worked example.

68
00:03:14,159 --> 00:03:17,769
Convert twenty two degrees and
thirty minutes into radians.

69
00:03:18,269 --> 00:03:19,169
Step one.

70
00:03:19,090 --> 00:03:23,462
Sixty minutes make one degree, so
thirty minutes is half a degree.

71
00:03:24,212 --> 00:03:25,112
Step two.

72
00:03:24,990 --> 00:03:29,634
The angle is twenty two and a half
degrees, which is forty five over two.

73
00:03:30,384 --> 00:03:31,372
Step three.

74
00:03:31,372 --> 00:03:33,694
Multiply by pi over one eighty.

75
00:03:33,694 --> 00:03:37,509
That gives forty five pi
over three hundred and sixty.

76
00:03:38,259 --> 00:03:39,238
Step four.

77
00:03:39,238 --> 00:03:43,106
Forty five goes into three
sixty exactly eight times.

78
00:03:43,106 --> 00:03:46,008
So the answer is pi by eight radians.

79
00:03:46,758 --> 00:03:50,585
Check: one eighth of one eighty
is twenty two point five.

80
00:03:50,585 --> 00:03:51,497
It matches.

81
00:03:52,147 --> 00:03:53,613
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:53,963 --> 00:03:56,375
Convert two radians into degrees.

83
00:03:56,375 --> 00:04:01,287
Many students multiply by pi over
one eighty, and get pi by ninety.

84
00:04:01,687 --> 00:04:04,304
But that rule takes you into radians.

85
00:04:04,304 --> 00:04:08,208
To come out of radians,
multiply by one eighty over pi.

86
00:04:08,208 --> 00:04:12,476
Two radians is about one hundred
and fourteen point six degrees.

87
00:04:13,126 --> 00:04:14,672
Where is this asked?

88
00:04:14,672 --> 00:04:19,684
In the PUC board exams: convert a
degree measure, often with minutes, into

89
00:04:19,684 --> 00:04:22,662
radians, or a radian measure into degrees.

90
00:04:23,262 --> 00:04:24,456
Exam level now.

91
00:04:24,456 --> 00:04:29,614
At four twenty, what is the angle
between the hands of a clock, in radians?

92
00:04:29,614 --> 00:04:30,858
Pause and solve.

93
00:04:36,258 --> 00:04:37,684
The answer is A.

94
00:04:37,684 --> 00:04:38,989
Pi by eighteen.

95
00:04:39,489 --> 00:04:42,377
The minute hand moves
six degrees every minute.

96
00:04:42,377 --> 00:04:46,429
At twenty past, it is one hundred
and twenty degrees from twelve.

97
00:04:46,929 --> 00:04:49,827
The hour hand moves half
a degree every minute.

98
00:04:49,827 --> 00:04:54,119
At four twenty, it is at one twenty
plus ten, which is one hundred and

99
00:04:54,119 --> 00:04:55,153
thirty degrees.

100
00:04:55,653 --> 00:04:57,450
The gap is ten degrees.

101
00:04:57,450 --> 00:05:02,188
Multiply by pi over one eighty,
and you get pi by eighteen radians.

102
00:05:02,788 --> 00:05:04,247
Here is your recap.

103
00:05:04,247 --> 00:05:05,746
Screenshot this card.

104
00:05:08,946 --> 00:05:11,783
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:05:12,183 --> 00:05:13,083
One.

106
00:05:12,723 --> 00:05:16,582
Convert one hundred and thirty
five degrees into radians.

107
00:05:17,482 --> 00:05:18,382
Two.

108
00:05:18,083 --> 00:05:21,654
Convert five pi by three
radians into degrees.

109
00:05:22,554 --> 00:05:23,454
Three.

110
00:05:23,138 --> 00:05:26,849
Why is it wrong to write pi
equals one hundred and eighty?

111
00:05:27,949 --> 00:05:32,279
Next time: Aarav's tyre valve goes
round, forwards and backwards.

112
00:05:32,279 --> 00:05:34,112
Can an angle be negative?

113
00:05:34,112 --> 00:05:36,374
We find out on the unit circle.
